export const SHORT =
  "Governments spend too much on science. The arts matter as well. I think this is a bad idea because culture is important to every country and people need it.";

export const FULL = `Governments in many countries are now directing more public money towards science and technology than towards the arts. Although this trend is understandable, I believe it is largely a negative development because it narrows education and weakens cultural life. This essay will explain why creative subjects deserve continued public investment.

Firstly, the arts develop skills that every modern economy needs. Students who study music, drama or design learn to think creatively, collaborate and communicate ideas clearly. For example, many successful engineers credit their artistic training for helping them solve problems in original ways. Moreover, cultural institutions such as museums and theatres attract tourists, which generates income and employment for local communities. In addition, creative education encourages empathy and cultural awareness, qualities that help young people work together in diverse workplaces and societies.

On the other hand, supporters of science funding argue that technological progress improves health, transport and communication. This is a valid point, and nobody can deny that research has saved millions of lives. However, scientific breakthroughs alone cannot give society a sense of identity or meaning. A country that invests only in machines risks producing citizens who are skilled but disconnected from their own heritage, and who struggle to express themselves beyond technical language.

In conclusion, reducing arts budgets in favour of science is a short-sighted policy. Governments should pursue a balanced approach in which both fields receive adequate support, because a healthy society needs innovation and imagination in equal measure. Consequently, I firmly believe that this development is negative.`;
